68K-UTILS -- clean-room OS-9/68000 utilitiesWHAT THIS ISReimplementations of Microware OS-9/68000 utilities, written from thepublished manuals rather than from any Microware source.  They exist so thatthis project can exercise, document and test behaviour that would otherwiseneed a licensed system disk to reach at all.At present there is one:    load    Load Module(s) from File into MemoryWHAT IS COMMITTED, AND WHAT IS NOTSRC/ is committed.  CMDS/ is not, and the reason is worth stating plainlyrather than leaving to be discovered.These are built with `cc -I`, which links against the shared `cio` C-I/O TRAPHANDLER instead of copying the C library into the module.  That is worth fourtimes the size -- load is 4216 bytes built this way and 16848 built the defaultstatic way -- and it is how OS-9's own utilities are built: stdio lives once, ina module every program shares, which is the entire reason trap handlers exist.The cost is a run-time dependency.  A cio-linked module must find cio throughits EXECUTION directory or die with    **** Can't install trap handler ****    **** cio ****before main() ever runs.  On an ordinary system disk cio is in CMDS and thisnever comes up; anywhere else it has to be put within reach.  tools/conformance.shinstalls cio beside load for exactly this reason, and installs both or neither.What is left in the module is still not ours to publish, which is why CMDS/ isgitignored: cstart.r and whatever of the C library survives -I are the vendor's,linked in as they are into anything a C compiler builds.  cio itself is thevendor's too and is borrowed from a system disk at run time, never copied intothis tree for keeps.THE WATERMARK -- FIXED AT THE SOURCE, 2026-08-27$OS9DISK/LIB/cstart.r IS NOW THE UNTAGGED ONE, so an ordinary `cc` produces aclean module and nothing has to be passed to get one.  The tagged original iskept beside it as LIB/cstart.r.tagged-orig; restoring it is a copy.  This iswhat makes a freeware rebuild come out clean without anyone remembering to.The rest of this section is why that was needed and how the replacement wasmade, because a binary swapped into a system disk with no account of where itcame from is worse than the problem it solved.THE WATERMARK, AND HOW TO NOT HAVE ONEcstart.r on a PERSONALISED system disk carries a banner naming the licensee --literally ">>>> from the disk of <name> <<<<", sitting in the code psect -- soevery C program built against it is stamped with that name.  For something beinghanded to a freeware collection that is the last thing you want.Only cstart.r carries it.  cio.l, clibn.l, math.l and sys.l were each checkedand are clean.It is not a compiler difference.  Measured with ident, cc/cpp/c68/o68/r68/l68are byte-identical across the disks here -- same size, same edition, same CRC.Nor is the unstamped /dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r an older or foreign cstart, which iswhat its file mtime suggests.  Host mtimes record when a file was COPIED; thebuild date lives in the ROF header at offset 12, and both of these are 1989:    /dd/LIB/cstart.r       1355 bytes, STAMPED     built 1989-06-05    /dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r   1274 bytes, unstamped   built 1989-04-07They are the same source.  cstart.a (@(#)cstart.a 2.1.8 2/23/89) carries thebanner as a plain data block at the head of the psect:    Author: dc.b ">>>>>>>>>>>>>>>>"            dc.b "from the disk of"            dc.b " <the licensee> "            dc.b "<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<<"a label nothing in the file references, preceded by seven dc.w $b0bd words --which are not filler at all: $B0BD reads BOBD, the author's own initials, asecond tag in the same block.  Both were removed.  Assembling that source with the four dc.b lines and the filler removedreproduces /dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r EXACTLY -- 1274 bytes, and the only bytes thatdiffer are the six-byte assembly date in the ROF header.  (The shipped stampedone was built from a revision carrying two filler words rather than seven,which is the whole of the 10-byte size difference.)So the unstamped cstart is not something to be wary of pairing with this disk'slibraries.  It is this disk's own cstart with the nameplate left off.The obvious move -- just link some other unstamped cstart -- does not work.The watermark-free copies from LATER releases (ansi_cstart.r and acstart.r at1380 bytes, the SDK's own at 1409) are all newer than this linker, whichrejects them outright:    l68: error - psect 'cstart_a' in file '...' created by assembler too new         for this linkerThe answer is a cstart this linker will accept, and the source supplies one.The replacement now installed in LIB was assembled from cstart.a with theAuthor block and the $b0bd words removed; it is byte-identical to thelong-standing /dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r apart from the six-byte assembly date, whichis as strong a check as this could have -- the result is a file that has beenin use since 1989.build.sh still takes either override, for a disk whose LIB has not been fixed:    tools/68k-utils/build.sh                        -> 4148 bytes, clean    export OS9CSTART=/dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r           # same result, explicitly    export OS9SDK=/path/to/a/disk/with/other/libs   # -> 4110, other librariesOS9CSTART names an untagged cstart directly, for a system disk whose LIB stillhas the tagged one.  Nothing is mixed: it is the same generation.OS9SDK points at a second disk carrying a DIFFERENT BUILD of the C libraries(clibn.l 35466 against this disk's 38523) -- not a newer cstart, and not anewer compiler.  It produces a slightly smaller module.  What it costs issmall and known: comparing the two clibn.l by exact symbol, it is missingexactly THREE functions -- fgetc, fputc and setvbuf -- and there is nothing init that this disk's library lacks.  getc and putc are macros in stdio.h and areunaffected.  But all three missing names are DECLARED in stdio.h, so a programcalling one compiles clean and fails at LINK time on an unresolved symbol.That is the only way this choice can bite, and it is why the message is worthrecognising.If /dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r is ever lost, it can be regenerated: assemble cstart.awith the Author block removed.  That is the reason to keep the source around --the unstamped binary stops being a mystery object and becomes something thetree can rebuild.Measured three ways from the same source: 4216 bytes stamped, 4148 clean viaOS9CSTART, 4110 clean via OS9SDK.  The build reports which it produced on everyrun, so a stamped module cannot be given away by accident.So the module is built on demand and never committed.  This matches the restof the repository: test/68k-live-verification ships .a and .c sources only,and the fifty prebuilt modules in test/68k-conformance/CMDS can ship exactlybecause every byte of them is hand-written assembly of ours.BUILDING    export OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9/system/disk   # /dd: the shell, and DEFS    export OS9SDK=/path/to/an/unstamped/toolchain  # optional, see below    #   ...or OS9CSTART=/dd/USR/DOG/cstart.r       # optional, see below    tools/68k-utils/build.shThat needs a licensed OS-9/68000 disk with the C compiler on it.  There is nohost cross-compiler for this target, so a machine without that disk cannotbuild these -- which is a supported state and not a failure.  Nothing in thetest suite depends on the result being there; see below.HOW load IS TESTEDCONF68K's t49 checks the claim the utility exists to satisfy: that after aload the module is in the module directory.  tools/conformance.sh installsload into the run when it has been built and leaves it out when it has not,so:    load and cio installable   t49 reports PASS -- the claim was checked    either one missing         t49 reports SKIP, carrying 221 (E$MNF)                               from F$ForkDOCS/expected records the SKIP, because that is what a fresh clone and CIsee, and the comparison in tools/conformance.sh accepts a PASS against arecorded SKIP without calling it news.  A FAIL is still news.  Seetest/68k-conformance/DOCS/claims.md, row t49.WHAT load IMPLEMENTSThe five options its v2.4 manual page documents:    load [<opts>] {<path>}      -?         function, syntax and options      -d         load from the data directory (Read_) rather than the                 execution directory (Exec_)      -l         print the pathlist the module was loaded from      -z         read the file names from standard input      -z=<file>  read the file names from <file>plus the PATH search the same page describes: when an Exec_ mode load of aname that is not an absolute pathlist fails, each colon-separated directoryin PATH is tried in turn.The shipped v2.4 binary answers -? with four more options that its own manualpage does not document -- -c=<color>, -d=<path>, -i and -s -- and none of themare here.  SRC/load.c says so at the top, and says why for each.Its messages, its -? layout and its exit status are matched to the shippedutility's, which was established by running that utility and reading what itprinted.  That is its interface, not its implementation.LINE ENDINGSSources here are CR-only, as OS-9 requires, for the same reasontest/68k-conformance/SRC is: a C source with LF endings reads to the compileras one enormous line.  If you edit these through a host tool, check with`flip -t` before you check anything else.GIVING load TO osk-freewareThat collection's own LICENSE already settles the shape of this: "The programswere compiled with Microware's cc, which links its cstart and C library -- thatis what a compiler is for."  A cc-built binary carrying cstart and clib is whatthe rest of the collection is.  It also ships cio, csl, csl020, math and math881with Microware's permission, so a -I build has its trap handler waiting for itthere.So what to hand over is a CLEAN build -- OS9SDK for preference, OS9CSTARTotherwise, either way with no licensee name in it -- together with SRC/load.c,since rather less than half of that collection has source and this is one morepiece that can.